<Project>

  <!--
    Warnings are errors for every .NET project in this repository.

    Why one file and not five <PropertyGroup>s: the same drift that bit the two clean
    scripts and the two build scripts bites build settings too — a property added to
    four of five csprojs silently leaves the fifth permissive, and nothing reports it.

    Why this is here at all: warning TUnit0015 ("Missing TimeoutAttribute cancellation
    token parameter") shipped in 9059512 and survived until now because nothing made
    it fatal. The C library already has a mechanical zero-warning rule (the `werror`
    preset); this is the .NET counterpart.

    Scope note — the BenchmarkDotNet question, answered by reading the project BDN
    generates for a run rather than by reasoning about it: BDN writes
    BenchmarkDotNet.Autogenerated.csproj under bin/<config>/net10.0/<name>-<job>-N/,
    which is inside this tree, but it sets <ImportDirectoryBuildProps>false</
    ImportDirectoryBuildProps> and an explicit <TreatWarningsAsErrors>False</
    TreatWarningsAsErrors> of its own. Measured on macOS/arm64: that project reports
    TreatWarningsAsErrors=False, so a benchmark run is untouched by this rule and needs
    no exemption here. Every hand-written project in the repo does inherit it (all five
    verified), which is the point.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One C# language version for every project in the tree, for the same drift reason as
    the rule above.

    This is a no-op while every project targets net10.0: that TFM already implies 14.0.
    It earns its keep the moment a project lowers or multi-targets its TFM, because
    LangVersion is *derived* from TargetFramework and the derived level is not the one
    this source was written against. Measured on macOS/arm64 with a netstandard2.1 leg:
    the derived level is C# 8, and the tree fails with CS8400 on every file-scoped
    namespace plus on the SDK-generated GlobalUsings.g.cs — i.e. <ImplicitUsings> breaks
    too, which is the non-obvious half. Pinning the language level here decouples it from
    the framework target, so a TFM change is a deliberate decision rather than a
    wholesale re-parse of the tree at a language level nobody chose.

    `latest` is the compiler's newest *released* major (not `preview`): a preview language
    version would let a feature land that the other two platforms' toolchains may not
    support for the same TFM. BDN's generated project opts out of this file entirely
    (see the scope note above), so a benchmark run is unaffected by this property too.

    Deliberately NOT set here: <Nullable> and <ImplicitUsings>, which are per-project
    decisions and are already stated in each csproj.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
